{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 240,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant for supporting PBS"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2520,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Around two and a half million years ago, a\nhuman-ish creature looked up at the sky confused"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9000,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at a constellation familiar to her."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11940,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "She was confused because its brightest star\nwas getting brighter, and quickly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over around a day, it blazed to become brighter\nthan the full moon, easily visible during"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21840,
    "dDurationMs": 540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "daytime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23040,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For weeks this young australopithecus and\nher people-your ancestors-wondered at this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29040,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new celestial body."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30000,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then it faded and vanished entirely, leaving\na void in the ancient constellation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37260,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Since then, the stars have shifted into patterns "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40200,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that would be as unrecognisable to \nyour great-times-a-hundred-thousand"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44220,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grandmother as her constellations would be to you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48240,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But maybe, relatively soon, astronomically\nspeaking, we will"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53100,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "share a celestial experience—the wonder and the \nterror of seeing a familiar star go supernova."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60780,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yad al-jawzā -  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66120,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the hand of the giant in Arabic\n- now bastardised by medieval monks and the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73080,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tim Burton film to Betelgeuse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75600,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s the bright red star on the shoulder\nof Orion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78300,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s a red supergiant—a star 11 times\nthe mass of our Sun and nearing the end of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83400,
    "dDurationMs": 420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83820,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The thing about supergiants is that we can’t\nknow how far along the death sequence they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89040,
    "dDurationMs": 500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89760,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Betelgeuse could have a 100,000 years, or\nits gamma ray-bright death flash may already"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96240,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be travelling the short 640 light years between\nus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Probably more like the former, but recently\nBetelgeuse has been teasing us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106200,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 2019 it dimmed dramatically for several\nmonths—which seemed foreboding, but we now"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112140,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "understand was due to it coughing up obscuring\nmaterial from its own atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117780,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It seemed to recover, but over the past several\nweeks the star has brightened, and is now"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123300,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "half again brighter than normal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125460,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sadly, at least for astronomers, this is probably\njust a fit as Betelgeuse settles back into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132120,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its regular 400-day brightness cycle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135420,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our best estimates give \nthe star between 10-100,000 "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139140,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years, and besides, a Betelgeuse supernova"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142500,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wouldn’t be close enough to damage the earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144860,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But these events remind us that our night\nsky and local stellar neighbourhood is not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151740,
    "dDurationMs": 900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a static thing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152640,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we know a very visible supernova must\nhave occurred around 2.7 million years ago—we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159060,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know it because we find a layer of unstable\nisotopes like iron 60—beneath the ocean"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164400,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "floor and in the Antarctic snow and even on\nthe Moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168420,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This must have been produced in a recent stellar\ncatastrophe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172080,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We also see that the Sun inhabits a bubble\nof relatively low density material that was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178200,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "carved out by supernova explosions over the\npast 20 million years or so."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183060,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Supernovae have lit up our sky recently on\nastronomical and geological timescales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189120,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how long before we get another?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191820,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which star will it be?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194040,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And should we wish for it?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195660,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start by talking a little about the\ndifferent types of supernova, because some"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200820,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are much much more dangerous than others—as\nin they kill ya from farther away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204780,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The deaths of massive stars result in type\nII supernovae—and I’ll come back to type"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210300,
    "dDurationMs": 500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211320,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We also call these core collapse supernovae,\nbecause they happen when the star’s core"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216540,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wait for it collapses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219660,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For a star like the Sun, when it’s done\nfusing hydrogen into helium in its core, it’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224640,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "puff out into a red giant, then go through\nan extra fit of helium burning, then shed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230640,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its outer layers non-explosively leaving behind\na burned-out ball of hot carbon and oxygen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236280,
    "dDurationMs": 1260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- a white dwarf."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238500,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But stars around 8 times the \nmass of the sun or higher "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242640,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have enough gravitational crush to fuse heavier"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246060,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "elements—all the way up to iron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248340,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At that point, fusion ends and layered core\nof heavy elements collapses under gravity,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254400,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stopping only when the innermost ball of iron\ntransforms into a neutron star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259140,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This releases an explosion of neutrinos, that\nare so numerous and energetic that they blast"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265800,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the surrounding layers back out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267240,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s the supernova."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268980,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many neutrons are also blasted out, slamming\ninto the infalling nuclei to produce more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273900,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "heavy elements—including the iron-60 that\nled us to our Pleistoscene supernova."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278700,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The vast majority of the energy of a supernova\nis carried away in neutrinos, but by the time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284520,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these reach us they’re diffuse, and just\ndo what neutrinos do—pass through everything."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290760,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If a supernova went off in our vicinity, our\nvast neutrino detectors would go crazy for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296820,
    "dDurationMs": 500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a bit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298200,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then the light would arrive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299640,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Energetic light—gamma rays, X-rays, as well\nas plenty of visible light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304140,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is when the star increases in brightness to\n10 to 100,000 times its original."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310500,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our atmosphere prevents the most dangerous\nphotons from reaching the ground, with most"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316020,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of them intercepted by our ozone layer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if the supernova is close enough, a lot\nof that ozone can be destroyed, and can take"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323400,
    "dDurationMs": 900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a long time to recover."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324300,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In that recovery period, a lot more ultraviolet\nradiation from our own Sun will reach the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330360,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ground, damaging surface life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332100,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Scientists estimate that the destruction of\n30-50% of the ozone layer would result in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337680,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a mass-extinction event."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339120,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in order to do that a supernova would\nhave to be within 15-20 light years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345540,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lagging behind the wave of neutrinos and light\nis the actual shock-front of the supernova,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351960,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which takes 100s to 1000s of years to reach\nus depending on distance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355380,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is potentially much more dangerous."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358320,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That shockfront is a wave of particles, kinetic\nenergy, and magnetic fields that leads the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364380,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expanding supernova remnant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366660,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The magnetic fields act as a giant particle\naccelerator, accelerating particles to near"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372660,
    "dDurationMs": 660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light speed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373320,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These become cosmic rays, and may ultimately\ncontain up to 10% of the energy of the supernova."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378660,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cosmic rays have an easier time penetrating\nthe atmosphere than do gamma rays, and so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384660,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a nearby supernova can bombard the planet\nwith a huge increase in this dangerous radiation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390780,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for thousands of years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392520,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the supernova doesn't have to be so close\nto us for its cosmic rays to be deadly—within"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399300,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "30 to 50 light years to trigger a mass extinction,\ndepending on the local interstellar medium"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404940,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the supernova type."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405900,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the real kill-zone for a supernova."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409200,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But they can still be dangerous from further\nout."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412140,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, the pleistoscene supernova is\nthought to have been 50 to 100 light years"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419040,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "away—just outside the kill zone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421860,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there is evidence that many animals living\nin shallow layers of the oceans went extinct"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427560,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at around that time, while deeper-dwelling\nanimals were spared."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431460,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This has been attributed to a massive increase\nin the muon flux from the supernova."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437580,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unlike most cosmic rays, this heavy cousin\nof the electron can penetrate some distance"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442980,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into water, causing damage to surface layers\nbefore being blocked."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447360,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cosmic rays can also alter atmospheric chemistry,\nincluding destroying ozone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453120,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2.7 million years roughly corresponds to\nthe beginning of the Pleistocene epoch—a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458640,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "period of cooler climate compared to the previous\nPlioscene epoch."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462660,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We also call it the Ice Age with its periodic\nglaciations, which may or may not have ended"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470100,
    "dDurationMs": 600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470700,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s highly plausible that the subtler effects "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473880,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of that ancient supernova \nkicked off the Pleistoscene."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477720,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So supernovae can influence the Earth even\nif we’re outside the kill zone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483300,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there are ways to extend the kill\nzone for an exploding star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487620,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a study that came out in April this year,\na team of astronomers investigated a new source"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492960,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of supernova unpleasantness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495120,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As I mentioned, the quick blast of gamma rays\ndoesn’t last long enough to do substantial"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500100,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "damage at any distance likely for a future\nsupernova."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505020,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in cases where the progenitor star was\nembedded in a relatively thick region of gas,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510060,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that gas can continue to radiate X-rays for\nhundreds to thousands of years after the initial"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515820,
    "dDurationMs": 1080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explosion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516900,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Such a supernova could bake our ozone layer\nto extinction-level depletion from much further"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523080,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distances than a regular supernova - up to\n150 Light years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527040,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other way to extend the kill-range is\nwith a hypernova, in which the gamma rays"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531900,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are beamed in a particular direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533400,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If that direction is us we see a gamma ray\nburst."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538020,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can see these from the other side of the\nuniverse, but if one occurs within several"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543420,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thousand light years it can significantly\ndeplete the ozone layer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547200,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are much rarer than regular supernovae,\nbut there’s evidence that we do get smacked"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552720,
    "dDurationMs": 1260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every billion years or so."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554820,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve covered gamma ray bursts in another\nepisode, so I won’t go into more detail"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558900,
    "dDurationMs": 240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559140,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re figuring out the prospects of\ngetting hit by a regular supernova."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564600,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which we can now do, because we know the size\nof the kill-zone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568560,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Within 30 or so light years for death by cosmic\nrays, or 150 light years in special cases"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574680,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for a long X-ray bake."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576780,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Betelgeuse is 640 light years away, so no\nproblem there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581580,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are currently just a handful of stars\ncloser to us than Betelgeuse that will one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586980,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "day go supernova."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588300,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The nearest is Spica at 220 LY."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591660,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Spica is a blue giant and is just big enough\nto eventually go supernova."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596520,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s still burning hydrogen in its core,\nso is nowhere near death."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600180,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s also too far to cause problems when\nthat inevitably happens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606420,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before you relax completely, there is an impending\nsupernova that’s closer than these."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612420,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just within the maximum range of dangerous\nat 150 light years we have IK Pegasi."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620460,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This isn’t a massive star that’s about\nto die."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622980,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s a regular hydrogen-burning star a bit more\nmassive and hotter than our Sun in a binary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629340,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbit with a white dwarf."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631140,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember white dwarfs?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632520,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remnants of stars not massive enough to go\nsupernova."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635400,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When the living star in the IK Pegasi system\napproaches the end of its life it’ll start"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640620,
    "dDurationMs": 720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to puff up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641340,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Its outer layers will start to stream onto\nwhite dwarf, which itself will grow in mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647040,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When it reaches a critical mass—the Chandresekhar\nlimit at 1.4 times the mass of the Sun, a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654060,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "runaway fusion reaction will rip through the\nwhite dwarf interior and it’ll explode as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658860,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a type 1a supernova."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661740,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If this were to happen at IK Pegasi’s current\ndistance, we’d get a celestial lightshow"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667500,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "comparable to the Pleistoscene supernova,\nand presumably the whales would have a bad"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673860,
    "dDurationMs": 500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675060,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fortunately all of this is a couple of billion\nyears away, and by then IK Pegasi will have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680940,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "drifted to a much safer distance of a few\nhundred light years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685320,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we seem to be safe for now, but if all stars\nare moving relative to each other, then eventually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692400,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a dying massive star or a feeding white dwarf\nwill find its way into the neighbourhood of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696780,
    "dDurationMs": 360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697140,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how long before this happens?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701220,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are different ways to estimate this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703020,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One is by seeing how often this has happened\nin the past."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706140,
    "dDurationMs": 7500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are historical, geological, and astronomical\nrecords of past supernovae in and around the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713640,
    "dDurationMs": 500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Milky Way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715080,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Historically, there was the one we saw in\nthe large Magellanic cloud in 1987, and the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720960,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one observed by Johanne Kepler in 1604, and\nseveral in ancient records, like the one recorded"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727980,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by Chinese and Arab astronomers in 1054 AD,\nwhich we now see as the Crab nebula."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734460,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Geologically there’s the Pleistocene supernova,\nand also similar evidence for another supernova"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740820,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at around the 7 million years ago."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743880,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Astronomically we see the local bubble, indicating\nmultiple supernovae in the past 20 million"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750000,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years or so, and in that bubble there’s\neven a couple of supernova remnants and neutron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754440,
    "dDurationMs": 480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754920,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can also look at the frequency of supernovae\nin other galaxies, which we observe relatively"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760080,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frequently because we’re actively watching\n1000s of galaxies for these things."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764880,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the last way to make this estimate is\njust by counting the massive stars in our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769380,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxy and using our understanding of stellar\nevolution to project a supernova rate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774420,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All this put together, we get that a supernova\nof one type or another goes off on average"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780780,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every 50 years inside the Milky Way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783540,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most of these are many thousands of light\nyears away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787260,
    "dDurationMs": 7860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Supernovae occur in the 15-20 light years\nneeded to directly roast our ozone layer on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795120,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the order of a billion years or so—a similar\ntimescale to being hit by gamma ray burst."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801240,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These may have never happened, but it’s\nalso possible that one or the other or both"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806580,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could have been responsible for some of the\nextreme mass extinctions in Earth’s long"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811440,
    "dDurationMs": 500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "history."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813360,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Within the 30-50  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814740,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light years of the cosmic-ray-death-zone,\nwe expect that to happen on the timescale"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818160,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a hundred million years or so, with a similar\nscale for the rarer but longer-range X-ray-bright"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824640,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supernovae."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825960,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These must have happened before, and will\nhappen again on a similar timescale."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829980,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We don’t know when, but not tomorrow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833280,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re currently in a region of the Milky\nWay disk with a low star formation rate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838440,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But when we enter one of the spiral arms that\nrate goes up, and so does the danger."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845760,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The next supernova that you’ll be able to\nsee with your own naked eye could well happen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850500,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in your lifetime, but it’ll be very distant—likely\njust a bright star where there previously"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855960,
    "dDurationMs": 660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wasn’t one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857280,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The next truly spectacular supernova will\nprobably be Betelgeuse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862140,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s not entirely impossible, but we’re\nunlikely to catch that one ourselves, despite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868440,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its recent rumblings."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869880,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s more likely that this spectacle will\nbe enjoyed by a distant future generation,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 875280,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who may be different from us as we are from\nthe australopithecus proto-astronomer with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882120,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whom we started this story."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884520,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For better or worse, we live in a temporarily\nquiet period, supernova-wise, but we see the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890940,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evidence and importance and frequency of these\nstellar cataclysms everywhere, from the irradiated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896820,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ocean floor to the supernova-blasted bubbles\nof our local interstellar space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903720,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905760,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Brilliant is an online learning platform for\nSTEM with hands-on, interactive lessons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909780,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Brilliant is for curious learners, both young\nand old, professional and inexperienced."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914640,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Brilliant courses teach you how to think and\nsolve problems with interactive lessons in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919260,
    "dDurationMs": 500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "STEM."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920520,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum Computing is one of the most exciting\nSTEM fields because it lets you solve very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925380,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hard problems using quantum mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928080,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in Brilliant’s Quantum Computing course\nyou’ll begin by learning the nature of computation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933420,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and qubits."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935040,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But rather than just focusing \non hairy mathematics, "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937920,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’ll see how it all works with hands-on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940380,
    "dDurationMs": 840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experimentation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942000,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As you progress, your knowledge will build\nso you can understand quantum cryptography,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946620,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum teleportation, as well as how algorithms\nwork in quantum computers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You'll develop intuition about how Quantum\nComputing works and a window into the kinds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957360,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of incredible physics problems it can help\nto solve."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960060,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To learn more about Brilliant, \ngo to brilliant.org/spacetime"
    } ]
  } ]
}
